+# BLOCKED: 1 ms SLA for `top_words()`
+
+## What is impossible
+
+`test_sla` requires `top_words()` to process the 4 MB reference corpus
+(600,000 words, 4,083,137 bytes) in **under 1 ms**, measured around the call
+with `time.perf_counter()`.
+
+That budget is below the cost of a single unavoidable pass over the input, so
+it cannot be met by any implementation of the stated contract, in pure Python
+or otherwise.
+
+## Why (measured on this machine, CPython 3.12.5 / macOS aarch64)
+
+Minimum work the function must do before it can count anything:
+
+| Operation | Best of 5 runs |
+| --- | ---: |
+| `text.lower()` (case-insensitive comparison, nothing else) | **1.49 ms** |
+| `text.lower().split()` | 18.4 ms |
+| `re.findall(r"[a-z']+", text.lower())` | 58.3 ms |
+| `Counter(text.lower().split())` | 56.3 ms |
+| `Counter(rx.findall(text.lower()))` + sort (implementation below) | ~98 ms |
+
+Just lowercasing the 4 MB input — one C-level pass that allocates a second
+4 MB string, required by the documented case-insensitive behavior — already
+takes ~1.5x the entire SLA, before tokenization or counting. Producing the
+answer requires at least reading and normalizing every character and then
+examining 600,000 tokens; at 1 ms that would be over 4 GB/s of end-to-end
+processing including Python object allocation, which is not attainable.
+
+The overhead is not in the original algorithm's linear list scans (those were
+replaced; see below). It is in the irreducible input traversal and token
+creation. No available C extension closes a ~50x gap here (numpy and pandas are
+not installed, and neither would make a 4 MB tokenize-and-count job run in
+1 ms).
+
+## What was done instead
+
+- Rewrote `top_words()` to use `collections.Counter` with a precompiled
+  regex. This removes the original O(unique_words) list scans per token and
+  is roughly an order of magnitude faster while preserving exact semantics
+  (case-insensitive letter/apostrophe tokens, ties alphabetical).
+- `test_basic`, `test_case_and_apostrophes`, and `test_ties_alphabetical`
+  pass.
+- In `test_sla`, the result-equality assertion passes; only the
+  `elapsed < 0.001` assertion fails, because the SLA is physically
+  unachievable.
+
+## What would unblock it
+
+One of:
+
+1. Relax the SLA to a measured achievable bound (the optimized implementation
+   needs ~0.1 s for this corpus; a realistic target would be, say, 150 ms), or
+2. Benchmark a warm cache/pre-tokenized corpus and exclude normalization, or
+3. Change the requirement from a per-call 1 ms bound to a throughput or
+   precomputed-index design.
+
+The test was not modified, skipped, or special-cased, and `top_words()` was
+not made to detect the reference corpus or otherwise fake the result.
